There can be a physical switch on the computer which switches to the system
special screen, which is implemented as a part of the operating system
kernel. There is also a light to indicate if it is currently active.

The system special screen is not a process, and all processes are suspended
while it is displayed. It also takes up the entire screen, and the keyboard
is used for the system special screen.

The system special screen can be used for such things as:

* View various status information.

* Take a screenshot of the entire screen that would be displayed if it was
not the system special screen, and is stored in a system screenshot memory.

* Low-level debugging.

* Manage low-level disk caching.

* Manage processes, including changing scheduling, suspending or
terminating them, dumping them to a partition for this purpose, etc.

* Relinking and discarding capabilities of a processing.

